The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if ((unsigned)rs->cont_offset > blocksize - min_de_size ||
    (unsigned)rs->cont_size > blocksize ||
    (unsigned)(rs->cont_offset + rs->cont_size) > blocksize) { ... }
if (rs->cont_extent) {
	bh = sb_bread(rs->inode->i_sb, rs->cont_extent);
}
Reason:
The existing check validates only `cont_offset` and `cont_size` but does **not** validate `rs->cont_extent` against the volume size (`s_nzones`). Consequently, an attacker can craft a Rock Ridge CE record with an out‑of‑range or adjacent‑filesystem block number, which is then passed directly to `sb_bread()`. This can lead to an information leak (via `readlink()` of SL records from read‑in buffers of other filesystems) or a NULL pointer dereference (`sb_bread()` returns NULL for out‑of‑range blocks, but the code does not handle the case where `bh` is NULL gracefully elsewhere).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if ((unsigned)rs->cont_extent >= ISOFS_SB(rs->inode->i_sb)->s_nzones) {
	printk(KERN_NOTICE "rock: corrupted directory entry. "
		"extent=%u out of volume (nzones=%lu)\n",
		(unsigned)rs->cont_extent,
		ISOFS_SB(rs->inode->i_sb)->s_nzones);
	ret = -EIO;
	goto out;
}
Reason:
This new check rejects any CE continuation whose `cont_extent` is outside the valid block range of the mounted ISO 9660 volume. By comparing `cont_extent` with the volume’s total number of zones (`s_nzones`), the fix ensures that `sb_bread()` will only be called with a legitimate block number, preventing both out‑of‑range reads and potential information leaks from adjacent filesystem data.

